  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe Ohio valley/southern Great Lakes low pressure system remains the \nforecast focus as it still presents a heavy rain threat today. A \npositive development is that evolution of the system helps confine \nheavy rain potential to about the southeast half of SE Mi, more so \nthe counties along/near the Canadian border. The area is narrowed \ndown by the northern stream mid/upper level trough digging from \nnorthern Ontario into the central Great Lakes while forcing strong \ncontraction of the Ohio valley trough. This results in very good \nmodel agreement on a much sharper deformation zone overhead that is \ncapable of heavy rainfall during the morning. The larger scale \nenvironment supports several hours of strong dynamic forcing within \nan unstable thermal profile, weak but still unstable, with a deep \nmoisture supply. Bulk parameters for elevated instability are weak \nbut favorable, such as 850 mb LI slightly negative in a moist \nadiabatic profile. Dewpoint at 700 mb in the 5-7 C range is also \nimpressive and reflects the warm rain efficiency suggested by PW \nhovering around 1.8 inches through the morning. The deformation band \nsolidifies through sunrise and is slow to move off a SW to NE axis \nover far SE Mi presenting a lengthy period of time for heavy rain \nand potential flooding. Consensus of regional and global models has \nevent total QPF in the 1-3 inch range while HREF 3-6 hr PMM mean QPF \nexceeds 3 inches. REFS PMM mean is less bullish and farther east to \nremind us there is still some uncertainty on the final band \nlocation. Hi-res versions of the NAM offer much higher QPF to also \nremind us there is some uncertainty on magnitude as well. Bottom \nline here is that small changes in the band location can result in \nbig changes to the rainfall outcome. Please see the earlier issued \nFlood Watch and the Hydro section below. \n\nThe rain band exits eastward by late afternoon as the mid/upper \nlevel trough and surface low shift to over Lake Erie/Ontario. \nModerate northerly wind trails the system across the central Great \nLakes which brings cooler and less humid air into SE Mi tonight and \nMonday. Guidance low temperatures drop into the mid 50s outside of \nmetro Detroit and return to around 80 Monday afternoon, right around \nnormal for early August. \n\nA large area of high pressure is shown to set up and dominate \nconditions across the Great Lakes for the early week period. Dry \nweather holds through Tuesday until the next low pressure system \napproaches from the Plains and central Canada. It brings a cold \nfront that stays in the picture from Wednesday through late week.",
